WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.910
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:10.340
So we'll continue the
discussion on QED.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.990
In the last video, we
looked at wave equations

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.230
and we discussed
Dirac equations.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.650
Now we want to look at solutions
to the Dirac equations.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.850
All right, so remember
that the overall goal now

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.260
is to find a description of
spin-half particles, which

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.560
we can then use in our
matrix element calculation

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.200
in order to get to
cross sections or decay

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.910
rates of particles.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.270
If you just ad hoc or
natural choice for a solution

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.660
would be a wave
equation, which is

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:53.200
a product of a spinor, which
depends on energy and momentum,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.620
and an exponent.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.540
So we have a free plane wave as
a solution to our free particle

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.300
base waveform.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.640
We have to show or
we have to make sure

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.730
that this wave equation
satisfies the Dirac equation

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:08.960
as shown here.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.870
Since the spinor depends only
on energy and momentum here,

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.600
it's rather simple to
write down the derivatives,

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.962
because they only
depend on the exponent.

00:01:19.962 --> 00:01:20.920
So we can do this here.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.820
And we find those solutions
here for the four components.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:31.750
We can rewrite this by putting
those derivatives here back

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.510
into the Dirac equation.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:39.250
What we find then here's this
simplified form for the spinor.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:42.020
Note that this does not
depend on derivatives anymore.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.150
So this is a rather simple form.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.380
And then we can study--

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.690
what happens now if they
have a particle at rest.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.790
So it further simplifies
the Dirac equation.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.580
It further simplifies
here to items E

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.650
times gamma 0, u-- that's
our spinor-- equal m times u.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:03.090
Since gamma 0 is a
diagonal, or is diagonal,

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.650
we can immediately find the
eigenstates to this equation.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.820
So we find four
different eigenstates,

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.790
and they are orthogonal.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.440
And you find that they
look very similar.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.660
So n here is just the
normalization factor,

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.730
which is the same for all four.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:24.090
And we find those four
different values here.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:29.520
You can find two with a negative
sign in the exponent and two

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.110
with a positive sign
in the exponent.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:34.620
Now this is for
particles at rest, fine.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.820
We can interpret those solutions
as positive and negative energy

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:43.230
states of a spin-half particles
or a particle with two spin

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.832
states.

00:02:44.832 --> 00:02:46.290
But now we want to
see what happens

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.630
if you have a particle
which is not addressed.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.300
So the way to approach
this is, so once, we

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.040
can just apply
Lorentz transformation

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.220
and see how the
solutions transform.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.530
But it is even easier
to just look directly

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:03.210
at the Dirac equations
for the spinor.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.370
So we start again from
our equation here.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.930
We just write this down.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:10.620
And then we rewrite the
equation using the gamma

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.240
matrices until we find those
factors p times gamma 1--

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.755
px times gamma 1, py
gamma 2, p3 gamma 3.

00:03:19.755 --> 00:03:25.280
Can we write this using the
Pauli matrices in this form?

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.940
OK, so what this gives us is
this coupled form of equations.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:38.690
So here we revised our spinor
as a two vector, uA and uB.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:41.220
And you find the coupled
form between those two

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:45.130
if we look at those
set of equations.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:45.880
Great.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.190
But this is rather
cumbersome and complicated.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:51.400
However, now we can try
to find the solutions

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:55.850
or try to find eigenstates
to the equation.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:58.510
We know that the solutions
are of this form here.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.400
That's how we started.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.980
If you then try to find
a specific [? state, ?]

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:09.910
you can start from the
simplest alternate solution,

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:13.840
which is uA equal 1, 0.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.310
And then just put this in here.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:18.399
And you find for u1
those solutions here.

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:19.660
And then you turn this around.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:23.930
For uB, you find 1 and 0 and
you find the other solution.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.250
So similarly as for
the solutions at rest,

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:29.500
we find here for our
spinors that there

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:35.250
is four different spinors,
which are independent.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.630
You can interpret
them now as, again,

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.900
the positive and
negative energy states.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:42.700
So if you then,
for example, say,

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.220
OK, let's make sure that
this is all consistent,

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.245
we want to see that
when the momentum is 0,

00:04:48.245 --> 00:04:49.870
you come back to the
previous solution.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:51.620
If you look at the
momentum, if they're 0,

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.410
those components are all here--

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.320
become 0, you find
the very same solution

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.800
as we had on the previous page.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.970
You can also ask
yourself what happens now

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:09.160
if you don't use this idea of
positive and negative energy

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:09.790
solutions.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:12.310
If you want to do
that and you define

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.340
that as all are either
positive for energy solutions,

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:17.680
and not two positive
and two negative,

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.330
you find that you
can divide them

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:21.670
as linear combinations
of the others,

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:23.990
so they're not
independent solutions.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.990
So in order to have four
independent solution

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:28.840
of the Dirac equations,
two have to be positive

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:31.780
and two have to be
negative in energy.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:36.070
All right, so I
recommend just trying

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.890
the exercise of playing
around with the Pauli matrices

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:40.360
and the gamma matrices.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.970
If you have not
seen this before,

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:45.940
it's not easy to
follow the algebra.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.620
But once you get
a hang of it, it's

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.900
actually not that complicated.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:54.020
In the next lecture, we look
at the solutions-- specifically

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.720
the solutions for
antiparticles a little bit more

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.670
and discuss interpretations
of those solutions.

